reac-pw (1.0.38) unstable; urgency=medium

  * libreac, libreac-transport and libreac-tests 1.9 are in range: the
    ceiling is the next minor (< 1.10). 1.0.37 pinned them below 1.9, so a
    channel carrying libreac 1.9.0 could not install it. The floor is
    unchanged.
  * A NIC the host uses is never bounced and never streamed at (#46): a
    wire carrying an address wider than link scope, a VLAN, macvlan or
    ipvlan child stacked on it, a bridge or bond it is enslaved to, or a
    default route out of it is the host's (reac_link_owner: three
    rtnetlink dumps, unprivileged, at most once a second per wire; an
    unreadable wire counts as the host's). A master on such a NIC refuses
    HOST_NIC instead of cycling the link, and the knock sends its stream
    rungs there as control knocks. The trade: a box that answers only the
    stream, straight on such a NIC, is not found there; through its own
    VLAN sub-interface it is a bare wire and is knocked on in full.
  * 1.0.37 was never published: this release carries its changes too.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Tue, 27 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0000

reac-pw (1.0.37) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A silent wire is knocked on instead of driven: a master stays off a
    wire where no box has been heard, knocks with a backed-off plan (two
    control knocks, then one hunting cycle, at most three) and streams only
    on the box that answered (#40, #31, #39). Until now the autodetection
    took a silent wire as its own and drove it at the stream rate for as
    long as it ran. Two limits remain on a NIC the host shares: the third
    knock is still one full cycle at the stream rate, and the wake ladder
    still cycles the link when nothing answers.

 -- Pau Aliagas <linuxnow@gmail.com>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:54:59 +0000

